---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, mcpJson, site } from '../content/site';

const title = 'Cursor memory between sessions, with MCP | hippo-memory';
const description =
  "Cursor memory between sessions: hippo keeps what earlier sessions learned in a local store, and Cursor's agent reads and writes it through MCP. No account.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Cursor" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro eyebrow="Cursor">
      <Fragment slot="title">Cursor memory <span class="text-gradient">between sessions</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        What Cursor's agent worked out in yesterday's session, such as which files matter and what broke, is gone in
        today's session unless something wrote it down.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hippo is one place to write it down: a local store that Cursor's agent reads and writes through MCP, shared with
        Claude Code, Codex and your other agents. No account, no API key.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Set it up">
      <Cmd lines={[site.installCmd, 'hippo init']} />
      <p>
        <code>hippo init</code> adds its instructions to <code>.cursorrules</code> if the project has one. Then give
        Cursor's agent the memory tools by adding hippo to <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>:
      </p>
      <Cmd lines={mcpJson} prompt={false} />
      <p>
        No global install? <code>"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "hippo-memory", "mcp"]</code> works too. The agent gets
        tools such as <code>hippo_recall</code>, <code>hippo_remember</code> and <code>hippo_outcome</code>; the
        <a class="link" href="/mcp/">MCP server page</a> lists all 13.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Bring your rules with you" alt>
      <Cmd lines={['# preview first, then import', 'hippo import --cursor .cursorrules --dry-run', 'hippo import --cursor .cursorrules']} />
      <p>
        <code>hippo import --cursor</code> takes a <code>.cursorrules</code> file, or one rule file from
        <code>.cursor/rules/</code>, and turns what is in it into memories, so lessons written for Cursor reach your other
        agents too.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="One store for every agent">
      <p>
        Use Claude Code on Monday, Cursor on Tuesday and Codex on Wednesday: same <code>.hippo/</code> store, same
        memories. A lesson Claude Code stored after a failed build is there when Cursor's agent opens the project.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cursor's rules are instructions you write: project rules in <code>.cursor/rules</code>, user rules, team rules and
        <code>AGENTS.md</code>
        (<a class="link" href="https://cursor.com/docs/context/rules" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cursor docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>,
        checked 2026-09-28). Keep those for standing instructions. Cursor also has Memories, introduced as a beta in
        Cursor 1.0 to remember facts from conversations per project
        (<a class="link" href="https://cursor.com/changelog/1-0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">changelog<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>).
        Hippo holds what the agent learns as it works, in a store your other agents share, and lets you mark a lesson
        wrong with <code>hippo outcome --bad</code> or replace a fact that changed with <code>hippo supersede</code>.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['How do I give Cursor memory between sessions?', 'Can I use hippo as an MCP memory server?', 'Where does hippo keep my data?'])}
      heading="Cursor questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
